edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050694 #edb/ref 1033 3361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050694 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5f79a517-5af5-5b02-b387-db32653ca27a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1033 3716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6768fffe-a644-5bd7-b35c-6b2cc2563786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177915 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177915 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177915 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177915 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186177919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177915 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1f6d5bae-0477-56a7-a550-3209e91d41b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177916 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{10}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177916 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177916 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bfd04950-8c76-5e5c-bd3b-57f33b66e8e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177917 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{10}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177917 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177917 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d33d012-f495-55e4-a694-7649d9356119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177918 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{26}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177918 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177918 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "470219b9-52ad-5f5d-85b1-f6487d2469f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177919 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f3d558e-c45c-5b6d-ba67-dbe452d8b64f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177920 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{26}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177920 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177920 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "77a8d59a-d4b3-536c-9bf3-7c9b158878e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179606 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179606 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179606 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179606 #edb/ref 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179607 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179607 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179607 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9d8e6c2-d346-52fb-918a-5d5c95cd986c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179607 #edb/ref 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{1}10{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179608 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179608 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6e/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C,$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179608 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "337d9d7e-5488-5e6d-af34-e01d22d3d0b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179608 #edb/ref 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{t^2}{4},\n\\qquad\nt\\in \\left[0,4\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/2b/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})=\\frac{t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (\\frac{t}{2})-(t)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179744 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179745 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179746 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1050 1200.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179867 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b8ced6b0-8e1a-57eb-9f2f-b4bd8eace852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180041 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180041 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180041 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180041 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i+xy\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14039085-c7d5-567d-835b-0c3eaccf0c38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180041 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =xy=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+18{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }26{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}26{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180504 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180504 #edb/ref 1061 35826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180504 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4fded59b-3b34-5c3a-b5bc-fbdf60f1028f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180504 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180505 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180505 #edb/ref 1061 35827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180505 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a52a1b21-2c56-5112-aa12-6e43086599bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180505 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180506 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180506 #edb/ref 1061 35829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180506 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb889341-22d5-52e6-9979-1fd814bc5cec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180506 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180507 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180507 #edb/ref 1061 35863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180507 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9195b98-bf60-5d80-a9f1-4dbe4c7ec614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180507 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180509 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180509 #edb/ref 1061 35901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180509 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6cc7967-4281-527a-8021-7768a792728f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180509 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180510 #edb/ref 1061 35902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180510 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7543ee2e-ca36-5209-8a6f-1fae02154a3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180510 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180725 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ be a *closed* curve in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nCalculating flux using this definition is often cumbersome because we need to calculate $\\mathbf n,$ the outward-pointing unit normal vector. \n\nIn practice, we often use the following alternative formula to calculate the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying this formula, we must choose a positively-oriented parameterization for $C.$ This formula will *not* work if $C$ is negatively oriented!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180725 #edb/ref 1065 5851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180725 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c74e835b-f210-5a77-8d6e-aef474c89522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180725 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180727 #edb/ref 1064 "As we've seen, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ through a piecewise-smooth, closed curve $C$ is a unique scalar number that represents the net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$ \n\nThe question now is, does the notion of flux exist for curves that are not closed?\n\nIndeed, it is possible to define the notion of flux through a non-closed curve. However, there is one crucial difference we need to bear in mind:\n\n- In the case of a smooth, closed curve, it is always clear which direction is \"out.\" Therefore, the outward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is unique at every point on $C.$\n\n- However, on a smooth, non-closed curve, there is no clear notion of \"in\" and \"out.\" Instead, we can choose between *two* normal vectors at any point on the curve, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d1/d193eb8109ead15de50c06a7bb0b654243173d882bd361f9fd34026aa23c1b5f.png)\n\nTherefore, when computing the flux of a vector field through a non-closed curve, we first specify the direction that the unit normal points and then measure the flux relative to that direction. \n\nFor example, for the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and curve $C$ below, one choice for the unit normal will give a positive number, and the other a negative number.\n\n![](images/4f/4f92dd5334b5836703857c081049f126d39c141e84d8e94171e338a162cf3cc4.png)\n\nFinally, to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across a non-closed curve $C,$ we can continue to use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** To apply this formula, we usually need to parameterize $C.$ It is *vital* that we pick a parameterization where the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is a $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180727 #edb/ref 1065 5869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180727 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e2794e26-620d-59e9-85de-a3bb49f6991a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180727 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Flux for Non-Closed Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180728 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now derive the following formula for the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that if $C$ is parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{y'(t)\\,\\mathbf i - x'(t)\\,\\mathbf j}{||\\mathbf r'(t)||},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $C$ is positively-oriented. \n\nSubstituting our expression for $\\mathbf n$ into our line integral definition of flux and using the fact that $\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t)|| \\,\\textrm d t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||})||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}})\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot ({y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P(x(t),y(t)){y}^{′}(t)-Q(x(t),y(t)){x}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180728 #edb/ref 1065 5870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180728 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cc71a06d-4832-5949-91b4-71cb0f8185ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180728 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Main Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421730 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421730 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421730 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "093b5f1d-7084-5cca-af48-56a7c276ea5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421731 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421731 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421731 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a0fbaf0-fbaf-5ffb-ba78-e300b9c3cb2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421732 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{25e}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1755e313-29bb-5235-930c-5480eea8e580" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421733 #edb/ref 1080 "-15\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421733 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421733 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "29d3016e-ea1f-57be-ad3f-89bd52e2b595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421734 #edb/ref 1080 "-30\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421734 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421734 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "29d80bea-8d5f-5d6e-9d57-a11322c402b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421735 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{7e}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421735 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421735 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a42ddb5-8533-50f3-9230-498c83bae1a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421736 #edb/ref 1080 "15\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421736 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421736 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2cd76549-ed5c-561