f 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421753 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421753 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eb22ea92-b3d9-59e8-9fe5-49b32e301c4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421754 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421754 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421754 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fbbebcc7-a768-5300-b557-75982c0cd1f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421755 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421755 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421755 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421755 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421755 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "04fdbb04-1f5e-524e-9c27-6e5e40173080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421756 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421756 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421756 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421756 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "452bd17e-660a-568a-8108-39d6d0770162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421757 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421757 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421757 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421757 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5f7eefb0-93b0-549d-9805-f25d2ffc89e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421758 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421758 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421758 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421758 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421758 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421758 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421758 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421758 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421758 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8411ac91-aa07-508a-9904-aa0a020877bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421759 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421759 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421759 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421759 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421759 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421759 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421759 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421759 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421759 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b62610d8-6a5e-55a5-8cf3-f983d7201b32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421760 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421760 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421760 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421760 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=100.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421760 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e7323f9-19dc-5d6f-a8c7-1664ab5ee0f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421760 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $5$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u+{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421761 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421761 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421761 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421761 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/62/62c8f6d7db345ce76bd164bb52c13e5edef9c04f18f4f07501c8694e7f64b554.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x={e}^{y}$ from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(e,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421761 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "735394db-bfa6-5cb2-9d7d-19fe3d01e994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421761 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(e,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/ba/ba6285008fd90835fdbf73203b76a0a01d713889d9939979aaec54bebd0a8c49.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}={t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)-({e}^{-t})\\cdot ({e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421762 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421762 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421762 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421762 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421762 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90df2f56-7b61-5a49-aa6d-4d0c58b35b14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421762 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,du-{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421763 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421763 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421763 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421763 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2007897-e589-5e81-a8ff-d3a65c150dee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421763 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+15{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =15(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =30\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421764 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421764 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421764 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/95e6a455863f0b4cefe184cca9ad86feaeff9f0702808db2d151f5d6615a04b8.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i-y\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421764 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddff35a9-8792-529b-9827-d26481dbe529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421764 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/cc/cc9d66f800a57f27673577cd3fd179b4cd6ff64585c5dc1a867317d8d592e485.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-y=-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})-(-\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
